        "The contract, verified:\n\n```tsx\n// a component = a function from props to element(s)\nfunction Badge({ tone, children }: { tone: 'good' | 'bad'; children: React.ReactNode }) {\n  return <span className={tone === 'good' ? 'text-emerald-600' : 'text-rose-600'}>{children}</span>;\n}\n\n// usage — everything inside the tags becomes props.children\n<Badge tone=\"good\">Shipped ✓</Badge>\n// props === { tone: 'good', children: <text>Shipped ✓</text> }\n\n// composition: a shell that accepts ANY content as a slot\nfunction Card({ title, actions, children }: CardProps) {\n  return (\n    <section className=\"rounded-lg border p-4\">\n      <header className=\"flex\">\n        <h3>{title}</h3>\n        <div className=\"ml-auto\">{actions}</div>   {/* slot 2: elements as props */}\n      </header>\n      {children}                                     {/* slot 1: the children prop */}\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n<Card title=\"Plan\" actions={<Button>Upgrade</Button>}>\n  <PriceTable />   {/* composed, not inherited */}\n</Card>\n```\n\nThe mental model (the interview core): render is a FUNCTION CALL TREE — React calls your component with props, your JSX calls more components, and the result is a tree of elements that React diffs. Two consequences: (1) props must be treated as immutable — the parent owns the values, and render purity (same props → same output) is what lets React skip or re-execute renders safely; (2) children is not special syntax, it's a prop — which means elements can be passed around, stored, conditioned: render the slot only when data loaded, or pass different trees for different states.\n\nComposition over inheritance (the React design answer you should give): instead of a BaseCard class extended by PricingCard, build Card as a SHELL with slots (children, actions, footer as element props) and specialize by what you pass in. Specialization pattern: a Card that pre-fills its own props then forwards the rest: const SidebarCard = (p) => <Card tone=\"side\" {...p} /> — two lines, no class hierarchy.\n\nThe typing layer (worth one line): props are a TS interface — children: React.ReactNode (NOT ReactElement — it must accept strings, numbers, fragments, arrays); when forwarding props, use ComponentProps<typeof X> to inherit rather than redeclare.",
