        "Why render purity is the load-bearing rule (the mechanism): React may call your component more than once per commit (StrictMode double-invoke in dev), skip it entirely (memo + same props), or re-run it after a failed commit — any of those misbehave if render has side effects (mutating props, writing to globals, firing requests as if in an event). The practical discipline: render computes UI from props/state; effects (useEffect, event handlers) do the world-interactions. When you need derived data, compute it in render (cheap) or memoize (useMemo) — never store props into state 'to modify them' (that's the copied-state anti-pattern: the copy drifts from the prop).\n\nchildren mechanics (the layer beneath the syntax): props.children is whatever you nest — a single element, an ARRAY of elements (React gives keys when it flattens sibling JSX), a string, or a function (render-props: {data => <List items={data}/>} — the parent controls WHAT renders, the child controls WHERE and with what data). The function-children pattern predates hooks and is still the cleanest API for cross-cutting containers (a <Theme children={(colors) => ...}> exposes its state without re-creating a context). Alternatives to cloning: cloneElement(children, { onClick }) injects props into an existing child — powerful but opaque; the composition-friendly form is a component prop the parent passes explicitly.\n\nThe component-boundary design question (the senior close): WHEN to split a component — the split rule is one-component-one-reason-to-change, and the practical triggers are (a) the same JSX appearing twice with different data (extract for reuse), (b) a region with its own state (its own update loop), (c) a region whose complexity hides the parent's story (extract to name it — the component is a NAMED abstraction), or (d) performance isolation (memo a hot region so parent renders don't touch it). Counter-signals: prop-drilling through 5 layers and 8-props-forwarding wrappers say the boundary is in the wrong place — hoist the state or move the component down.",
