      speakable:
        "Each bug with its verified fix:\n\n```tsx\n// 1. SIBLINGS OUT OF SYNC — each had a copy; LIFT to the common parent\nfunction Parent() {\n  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');          // ONE owner\n  return (\n    <>\n      <Search value={query} onChange={setQuery} />   {/* props down */}\n      <Results query={query} />                      {/* same source — cannot drift */}\n    </>\n  );\n}\n\n// 2. TODO DIDN'T UPDATE — mutation kept the reference\nsetTodos(todos.push(item));          // WRONG: push returns a NUMBER, same array\nsetTodos([...todos, item]);          // RIGHT: new array reference → Object.is differs → render\nsetTodos(t => [...t, item]);         // BEST: functional form — no stale-closure risk\n\n// 3. HANDLER FIRES DURING RENDER — you CALLED it\n<button onClick={save()}>Save</button>       // WRONG: save() runs during render\n<button onClick={save}>Save</button>           // RIGHT: pass the function\n<button onClick={() => save(row.id)}>Save</button>  // with args: wrap in an arrow\n\n// 4. SEARCH SHOWS OLD RESULTS — a race; guard by epoch or abort\nconst epoch = useRef(0);\nasync function search(q: string) {\n  const mine = ++epoch.current;\n  const res = await fetch(`/api/search?q=${q}`);\n  if (mine !== epoch.current) return;          // a newer call superseded mine — discard\n  setResults(await res.json());                // only the NEWEST result renders\n}\n```\n\nThe reasoning per bug (the interview structure):\n1. Two components each owning the same fact = two truths — they WILL diverge. One owner (lowest common parent), props down, events up. If the parent is far, a store or context owns it — never two copies.\n2. React's render gate is reference equality on state — the array you mutated IS the last render's array; nothing changed as far as the diff can tell. Immutable updates create a new reference; the functional form also protects you when updates batch and the outer variable is stale.\n3. JSX attributes are EXPRESSIONS evaluated during render — save() with parens is evaluated the moment React builds the element. The value of onClick must be a function that React calls LATER on the actual click.\n4. Networks don't order responses — a slow query 1 resolving after fast query 2 wins the race and paints stale data. The guard pattern (ignore responses whose ticket isn't current) makes the LAST request the only one that can render. AbortController additionally stops the work entirely (and aborts fetches, saves bandwidth).",
      flow:
        "flowchart TD\n    SYM{symptom} -->|siblings disagree| S1[two copies of the same state]\n    S1 --> F1[lift to the lowest common parent — one owner]\n    SYM -->|UI didn't update| S2[mutated state — same reference]\n    S2 --> F2[new array/object or functional setState]\n    SYM -->|runs on render not click| S3['onClick={fn()} — called during render']\n    S3 --> F3['pass fn, or arrow-wrap when args needed']\n    SYM -->|results are old| S4[race: slow first response overwrites fresh]\n    S4 --> F4[epoch guard + AbortController — only newest renders]\n    DELAY[adding setTimeout delays] -.masks, never fixes.-> S4",
      deep:
        "The stale-closure family (why bug 2's functional form is the deeper answer): every render closes over ITS OWN props/state — a handler created in render N sees render N's values forever. When events fire later (a delayed save, an interval, an async callback), they may act on a snapshot. The functional update (setTodos(t => [...t, item])) receives the CURRENT queue state from React, not the captured one — it's the fix whenever the next value depends on the last value. Same family: useEffect deps that omit the used variable (the effect closes over the old one forever); the eslint exhaustive-deps rule exists to catch precisely this.\n\nThe sibling-state ladder (bug 1's full toolbox): (1) lift to parent — the default, works until the tree grows; (2) context — cross-cutting values (theme, auth) without drilling, but every consumer re-renders on change; (3) component-level store slices (Zustand/Jotai) — subscribe by selector so only the components reading the changed slice render; (4) URL state for shareable/survivable facts (filters, tabs, wizard steps — the search-params pattern). The diagnostic question for ANY sync bug: 'who owns this fact?' — if the answer is more than one component, that's the bug.\n\nThe race-condition toolbox in order of preference (bug 4's professional close): (a) AbortController — cancels the previous request at the transport layer (fetch takes signal; the aborted fetch rejects with AbortError you must catch-and-ignore); (b) epoch/sequence tokens — language-level, works when you can't abort (websockets, non-fetch APIs); (c) request serialization (a queue that runs one search at a time) — correctness over freshness, right when the backend can't handle concurrent queries; (d) server components/streaming (React 18 useTransition + startTransition for non-urgent updates keeps the OLD UI visible while the new one loads — 'keep showing stale rather than flash empty' is itself a UX pattern). The interview close: 'guard by identity (epoch), cancel by transport (abort), and let the framework decide urgency (transition) — in that order.'",
